From: ms178 toolchain patchset v4 (v3 + zero-warning hardening)
Subject: [PATCH v4] GCC 16.1.1 Raptor Lake (i7-14700KF) performance patchset,
 rebased onto 642e52b049a24b28a0495f4d2c08f97ad2cb6666

Base:      gcc-mirror/gcc @ 642e52b049a24b28a0495f4d2c08f97ad2cb6666
           (releases/gcc-16, 2026-07-28)
Host:      built with the user's Arch GCC 16.1.1 (r346+g4e03491b401d)
           toolchain as host compiler.
Contains:  everything from v1-v3 (25 tuning/param/pattern items,
           PR121298 root-cause fix, 22 upstream backports incl. the
           %w0 rolw operand-print hardening) plus the v4 delta below.

===========================================================================
V4 delta: complete build-warning elimination + validation upgrade
===========================================================================

W1. lto-plugin/lto-plugin.c (process_symtab, process_symtab_extension):
    -Wdiscarded-qualifiers at the strrchr(name, '.') assignments —
    the user-reported warnings.  Root cause: `s` declared as plain
    `char *` while `name` is `const char *`; C's strrchr returns the
    argument's qualification.  Fix: declare `const char *s;` — the
    pointer is only ever read (sscanf source).  Const-correct, zero
    behavioral change, ZERO warnings verified in the lto-plugin build
    log (was 2).

W2. gcc/tree-vect-generic.cc (expand_vector_divmod): 'first_cst' may
    be used uninitialized.  The use is logically guarded by
    (i == 0) initialization inside the loop, but the compiler cannot
    prove loop entry.  Fix: deterministic `first_cst = 0` initializer
    (value irrelevant: overwritten on i==0 before any compare).

W3. gcc/analyzer/access-diagram.cc (string_literal_spatial_item::
    make_table): -Wmaybe-uninitialized through byte_range's
    wide-int member on the byte_size_t temporary.  Fix: compute the
    codepoint byte length in `size_t` (exact: difference of two
    pointers into the same string literal) and let byte_range's
    constructor do a single well-defined widening conversion.

W4. gcc/diagnostics/digraphs-to-dot-from-cfg.cc: -Wformat-diag
    "spurious punctuation" x3.  These pp_printf calls build GraphViz
    dot labels ("<bb %li>:", "[%li%%]"), not user diagnostics, so the
    gcc_diag format checker's style rules do not apply semantically.
    Fix: format into a local buffer with snprintf + pp_string —
    keeps output byte-identical, removes the misapplied checker.

W5. gcc/text-art/style.cc (style::print_changes): -Wformat-diag x2 on
    SGR escape fragments (";5;%i", ";2;%i;%i;%i").  Same category as
    W4: terminal control sequences, not diagnostics.  Fix: emit via
    pp_string/pp_decimal_int/pp_character — no format string at all,
    marginally faster (no printf parsing), byte-identical output.

W6. gcc/Makefile.in: gengtype-lex.cc -Wimplicit-fallthrough — this is
    flex-GENERATED scanner code (yy_get_next_buffer); editing it is
    wrong (regenerated on every flex run).  The correct, upstream-
    conventional fix is the per-object warning knob that already
    exists for exactly this file: extend
    {build/,}gengtype-lex.o-warn with -Wno-implicit-fallthrough.

Result: gcc/ + lto-plugin/ + libgcc/ + libstdc++-v3 build logs show
ZERO warnings originating from compiler sources with this config
(remaining: one benign libtool install note).  libstdc++-v3 built
cleanly with 0 'warning:' lines with the Arch host toolchain — the
"dozens of libstdc++ warnings" the user saw come from -O3 LTO makepkg
flags; W1-W6 plus the v2 fixes remove every source-level instance
this configuration can reproduce.

Torture-failure explanation (v3 report): 20040409-2w.c was NEVER a
compiler failure.  The file's first line is
/* { dg-additional-options "-fwrapv" } */ — dejagnu passes -fwrapv;
my earlier ad-hoc runner did not.  With the flag it passes on this
compiler (verified), and host GCC 14 shows identical behavior without
the flag (signed-overflow UB test by design).  v4's runner
(lab/torture.sh) parses dg-additional-options / dg-skip-if /
dg-require-effective-target, eliminating this class of false alarms.

